ved  as virions and plants we can hypothesize  that host specificity must then be  encoded in the read-through domain by  the process of elimination we've also  demonstrated for the first time that PIR  successfully identifies direct  functionally relevant interactions  between Polara viruses and their hosts  this is also not trivial we knew a very  few direct host virus interactions  previously and finding these  interactions was a matter of luck or  screening using yeast two-hybrid or  other high-throughput approaches which  are very time and resource intensive and  don't always identify interactions that  are functionally relevant not only have  we found many new host interactions to  explore but we've also made a huge  technological leap forward in the field  finally i didn't show all of the data  for this but in my biggs experiments I  found that knocking out most of the host  proteins that we identified as  interacting with Galera viruses by PIR  actually increased valera virus titer  this was a really surprising results  because we were expecting to find at  least an equal number of interactions  that are important for virus replication  or movement as for defense there are a  couple of ways that we can explain this  it's possible that plants really just  throw everything that they have at  viruses to try to prevent replication  and so most host virus interactions  actually represent the plant's attempts  to defend itself alternatively we know  that circuitous is that the circulat of  transmission the mode of Calero viruses  requires prolonged a third feeding for  acquisition and transmission while it's  beneficial for viruses with other  transmission modes to sacrifice their  hosts in an effort to produce as many  baryons as possible Polara viruses must  keep their hosts alive and attractive to  affix maybe the reason we see an  increase in tighter when we knock out  most in our host interaction partners is  because the function of these  interactions is actually to limit virus  replication to wrap things up real quick  I want to talk briefly about some  potential future applications and  impacts for this research I'm really  interested to see how these data could  be applied to other non model Polara  virus species including some of the  newer emerging viruses these types of  data could also be useful for the  development of interdiction molecules  which are molecules specifically  designed to interfere with biological  process Angela Cruz a grad student in  our lab is actually working on the  development of interdiction molecules to  prevent transmission of the bacteria  that causes citrus greening disease so  if you'd like to learn more about that  then you can look for her papers and  seminars I also think that this  technology has some potentially  interesting applications for gene  editing and particularly in the  development of resistant cultivars so  this is a paper from 2011 detailing the  development of potato cultivars  resistant to three strains of potato  virus y which is a podium is thats one  of the most important potato pathogens  today like polio viruses PBY has a  single-stranded RNA genome and encodes a  viral genome leaked protein or vpg which  binds to the five prime end of the viral  genome DPG then binds to a host  elongation factor in this case  for ii to recruit a host ribosomal  complex to initiate production of viral  proteins previous screens had identified  AI f-4e alleles and other plant species  which were fully functional but to  contain small polymorphisms that  prevented binding to vpg conferring  resistance to po d viruses in the study  the authors use targeted mutagenesis to  transfer those polymorphisms in the  potato the resulting transgenic lines  are numbered here on this graph with the  wild-type and other control lines on the  far right you can see that all of the  transgenic lines were almost completely  resistant to all three tested strains of  PBY whereas the controls were highly  susceptible  I believe that PIR guided modeling has  the potential to achieve the same end  result for polio viruses but in a much  shorter time frame we have already  established that PIR successfully  identifies functionally relevant host  virus interactions which is the first  step in developing a resistance strategy  like this we can also use PIR data to  model the interaction between the host  and viral proteins predicting where the  viral protein binds if this binding site  is located in an area that can be  mutated without affecting the host  proteins native function we can perform  gene editing to knock out the  interaction without affecting the  proteins native function this is  especially important because many of the  host proteins we found interacting with  kill our V and T u IV have important  roles in metabolism and development and  can't be knocked down or out without  causing significant and sometimes lethal  effects and this is only the beginning  now that we finally have the ability to  more directly explore structural biology  and protein protein interactions we can  try to tackle some of the oldest and  most important questions in Lutie  ovarian biology how do colera viruses  interact with their hosts can we  interrupt virus replication or movement  what's the structure of the polio virus  very on and what governs its assembly  can we design molecules to prevent  assembly or disrupt host virus  interactions  how do Polara viruses cause symptoms and  yield losses in host plants can we use  this information to reduce the impact of  these viruses and finally atha  transmission is largely a black box  application of PIR to a papyrus  complexes could greatly helped us to  understand circulat of transmission of  these and other viruses so overall I  think these data and this technology are  really exciting and give us the ability  to leap years ahead in our understanding  of klara virus biology and I hope I've  convinced you of that too before I